          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We Distribute, Yet Things Multiply"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.087,
            0.693,
            0.181
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Expand the following products. (i) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( 3 + u ) \\; ( \\nu - 3 )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (ii) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\textstyle { \\frac { 2 } { 3 } } \\left( 1 5 + 6 a \\right)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (iii) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( 1 0 a + b ) \\; ( 1 0 c + d )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (iv) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( 3 - x )   ( x - 6 )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (v) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\left( - 5 a + b \\right) \\left( c + d \\right)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (vi) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( 5 + z )   ( y + 9 )"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.185,
            0.801,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Find 3 examples where the product of two numbers remains unchanged when one of them is increased by 2 and the other is decreased by 4."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.817,
            0.178,
            0.904,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.817,
                0.178,
                0.904,
                0.23
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.252,
            0.757,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Expand (i) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( a + a b - 3 b ^ { 2 } )   ( 4 + b )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and (ii) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( 4 y + 7 ) \\; ( y + 1 1 z - 3 ) ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.281,
            0.849,
            0.358
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Expand (i) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( a ~ - ~ b ) \\quad ( a ~ + ~ b )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , (ii) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( a ~ - ~ b ) \\quad ( a ^ { 2 } ~ + ~ a b ~ + ~ b ^ { 2 } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and (iii) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(a - b)(a^{3} + a^{2}b + ab^{2} + b^{3})"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , Do you see a pattern? What would be expanding?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.378,
            0.778,
            0.4
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fast Multiplications Using the Distributive Property"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.409,
            0.848,
            0.448
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The distributive property can be used to come up with quick methods of multiplication when certain types of numbers are multiplied."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.462,
            0.642,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When one of the numbers is 11, 101, 1001, ...",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.494,
            0.846,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Use the following multiplications to find the product of a number with 11 in a single step."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.543,
            0.349,
            0.561
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(a) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 8 7 4 \\times 1 1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.577,
            0.542,
            0.704,
            0.562
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(b) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 6 7 8 \\times 1 1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.566,
            0.49,
            0.586
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us take the first multiplication."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.328,
            0.589,
            0.716,
            0.609
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_7_equation_14.jpg",
          "content": "3874 \\times 11 = 3874 (10 + 1) = 38740 + 3874"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.457,
            0.627,
            0.52,
            0.662
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "38740 3874"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.701,
            0.539,
            0.72
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Notice how the digits are getting added."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.72,
            0.847,
            0.776
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us take a 4-digit number dcba, that is, the number that has d in the thousands place, c in the hundreds place, b in the tens place and a in the units place."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.336,
            0.775,
            0.662,
            0.794
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_7_equation_18.jpg",
          "content": "d c b a \\times ( 1 0 + 1 ) = d c b a \\times 1 0 + d c b a ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.802,
            0.314,
            0.819
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This becomes"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.356,
            0.82,
            0.634,
            0.876
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_7_equation_20.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{\\begin{array}{ccccc}d & c & b & a & o \\\\+ & d & c & b & a \\\\\\hline& d & (c+d) & (b+c) & (a+b) & a \\\\\\end{array}}{}"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
